/tmp/tmp.8GN93Q6mH1/main.tex:475:Observe that every $u\in V(G)$ is spanned by both branchings, with the exception of vertices in $B=\{(T_i,2),(F_i,2)\mid i\in [n]\}$. However, these can easily be spanned in the second snapshot since $\{(g,2),(r,2)\}$ is complete to $B$.
/tmp/tmp.8GN93Q6mH1/main.tex:477:Now, let ${\cal B}_1,{\cal B}_2$ be two edge-disjoint vertex-spanning branchings. Because each $a_i$ can only be reached at the first snapshot, it is reached by exactly two paths from $\{(g,1),(r,1)\}$, one of them going through $(x_i,1)(T_i,1)$ and the other through $(x_i,1)(F_i,1)$. We then put $x_i$ as true if and only if $(x_i,1)(T_i,1)$ is in branching ${\cal B}_1$. Now, consider clause $c_i = (x_{i_1}\vee x_{i_2}\vee x_{i_3})$. Since all the paths from the roots to $c_i$ go through the temporal edges $(x_{i_1},1)(T_{i_1},1)$, $(x_{i_2},1)(T_{i_2},1)$, and $(x_{i_3},1)(T_{i_3},1)$, and $c_i$ must be spanned by ${\cal B}_1$ and ${\cal B}_2$, we get that at least one of these edges is in ${\cal B}_1$, and at least one in ${\cal B}_2$, which implies that at least one of $x_{i_1},x_{i_2},x_{i_3}$ is true, and at least one is false, as desired.
