%"Path problems in Temporal graphs":https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol7/p721-wu.pdf. They use temporal edges as we do, but the vertices are eternal. Their definition of temporal path is inappropriate since it is defined as a sequence of static vertices, and the temporal edges cannot be retrieved from this. They then define earliest arrival, latest departure, fastest (difference between ending time and starting time is minimized), and shortest (sum of edge durations is minimized). They then present algorithms to find such paths starting (or ending in the case of latest departure) at a given vertex $x$ to (or from) all other vertices of $G$.

